    englishMeaning: "Neither the hosts of celestial gods nor the great sages know My true origin; for I am the primal source and beginning of all the gods and all the great sages in every way.\n\nPhilosophical Commentary: Sri Krishna opens the theological heart of Chapter 10 with a bold declaration of divine transcendence. 'Prabhavam' encompasses His divine advent, primordial nature, and ultimate identity—and He states that this cannot be comprehended even by the greatest celestial deities (sura-gaṇāḥ) or the wisest cosmic sages (maharṣayaḥ). This is not a boastful claim; it is an ontological fact. The Creator cannot be fully known by the created, for knowing requires a vantage point beyond the knower's own existence. Gods like Indra, Brahma, and Varuna, and cosmic sages like Vasishtha and Bhrigu—all are themselves expressions and emanations of the One Divine Reality. A wave cannot fully comprehend the ocean that gave it rise.",
    lifeLesson: "True wisdom begins with the humility of recognizing how little we actually know. The greatest sages and most brilliant minds have only glimpsed the edges of cosmic truth. Approach the infinite with wonder, openness, and reverence rather than intellectual arrogance."
